{"reason": "The initial plan identified severe overfitting (training loss 0.0230 vs validation loss 0.7265 at epoch 7) due to excessive model capacity. The proposed changes—multi-scale feature fusion and layer-wise learning rate decay—directly address overfitting by constraining the transformer and enriching representations. Historical experiments show that hybrid models combining feature-engineered inputs with DeBERTa can improve robustness, but they also risk increased complexity. The current code already has a strong hybrid pipeline; the overfitting stems from the full DeBERTa backbone being fine-tuned. By applying layer-wise decay and freezing bottom layers (model_design) and adjusting the training loop to handle the new scheduler (training_evaluation), we can stabilize training. Data processing already includes rich features; no changes needed there.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the HybridAuthorshipModel to implement multi-scale feature fusion: replace the single [CLS] token from the last hidden layer with a learned weighted average of the last 4 hidden states from DeBERTa. This involves setting output_hidden_states=True, extracting the last 4 layers, applying a per-sample learnable attention mechanism (linear layer + softmax) to compute weighted average across layers, then mean pooling the sequence dimension to get a richer document embedding. Also implement layer-wise learning rate decay: freeze the first 4 layers (embeddings + first 3 transformer blocks) by setting requires_grad=False for those parameters, and apply a linear decay factor from 1.0 (top layer) to 0.5 (bottom layer) for the remaining layers. The stylo projection and classifier remain unchanged. These changes reduce effective trainable parameters and leverage multi-level features to combat overfitting.", "training_evaluation": "Update the optimizer setup to apply differential learning rates per layer as per the new model design. After creating the model, iterate over named parameters to assign learning rates: for frozen layers, no parameters; for unfrozen DeBERTa layers, compute a decay factor based on layer index (linear from 0.5 to 1.0); for head parameters, use a fixed higher LR (2e-4). Ensure the scheduler and gradient accumulation remain compatible. In the training loop, verify that gradients are not computed for frozen layers. Increase early stopping patience to 7 to allow slower convergence. Monitor validation log-loss and save best model. This adjustment ensures the refined architecture is trained correctly.", "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "No changes needed. The current pipeline already extracts rich stylometric and n-gram features, which are used in the hybrid model. Overfitting is addressed by model modifications, not data changes."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identified severe overfitting (training loss 0.0230 vs validation loss 0.7265 at epoch 7) due to excessive model capacity. The proposed changes—multi-scale feature fusion and layer-wise learning rate decay—directly address overfitting by constraining the transformer and enriching representations. Historical experiments show that hybrid models combining feature-engineered inputs with DeBERTa can improve robustness, but they also risk increased complexity. The current code already has a strong hybrid pipeline; the overfitting stems from the full DeBERTa backbone being fine-tuned. By applying layer-wise decay and freezing bottom layers (model_design) and adjusting the training loop to handle the new scheduler (training_evaluation), we can stabilize training. Data processing already includes rich features; no changes needed there.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the HybridAuthorshipModel to implement multi-scale feature fusion: replace the single [CLS] token from the last hidden layer with a learned weighted average of the last 4 hidden states from DeBERTa. This involves setting output_hidden_states=True, extracting the last 4 layers, applying a per-sample learnable attention mechanism (linear layer + softmax) to compute weighted average across layers, then mean pooling the sequence dimension to get a richer document embedding. Also implement layer-wise learning rate decay: freeze the first 4 layers (embeddings + first 3 transformer blocks) by setting requires_grad=False for those parameters, and apply a linear decay factor from 1.0 (top layer) to 0.5 (bottom layer) for the remaining layers. The stylo projection and classifier remain unchanged. These changes reduce effective trainable parameters and leverage multi-level features to combat overfitting.",
    "training_evaluation": "Update the optimizer setup to apply differential learning rates per layer as per the new model design. After creating the model, iterate over named parameters to assign learning rates: for frozen layers, no parameters; for unfrozen DeBERTa layers, compute a decay factor based on layer index (linear from 0.5 to 1.0); for head parameters, use a fixed higher LR (2e-4). Ensure the scheduler and gradient accumulation remain compatible. In the training loop, verify that gradients are not computed for frozen layers. Increase early stopping patience to 7 to allow slower convergence. Monitor validation log-loss and save best model. This adjustment ensures the refined architecture is trained correctly.",
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "No changes needed. The current pipeline already extracts rich stylometric and n-gram features, which are used in the hybrid model. Overfitting is addressed by model modifications, not data changes."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show high validation log-loss (0.474) and training stagnation due to frozen backbone and static learning rate. The initial plan proposes unfreezing with discriminative learning rates and SWA/cyclic LR to improve convergence. Historical successful experiment 1 used a hybrid model with feature engineering but suffered from wasted DeBERTa potential; experiment 2 fine-tuned DeBERTa end-to-end and achieved better results, validating the approach. Thus, the most promising modifications are in 'model_design' (unfreeze backbone with discriminative LR) and 'training_evaluation' (replace scheduler with OneCycleLR+SWA). The data processing is already correct and unchanged.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify SpookyAuthorClassifier to unfreeze DeBERTa backbone layers with discriminative learning rates: unfreeze last 6 layers (lr=1e-5), middle 12 layers (lr=5e-6), bottom 6 layers (lr=1e-6), keep custom head at 5e-5. This allows the pretrained model to adapt to author-specific stylistic patterns (e.g., Poe's Gothic vocabulary, Lovecraft's cosmic horror), as seen in historical experiment 2 where full fine-tuning improved performance. Implementation: set requires_grad selectively by layer index using backbone's layer list.", "training_evaluation": "Replace cosine scheduler with OneCycleLR (max_lr=2e-5 for backbone, pct_start=0.3, final_div_factor=1e3) and add Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) starting at epoch 15 with swa_lr=5e-6, snapshot every 5 epochs. This addresses training stagnation (LR constant at 2e-5) and validation oscillation, as historical experiment 2 used a warmup scheduler successfully. OneCycleLR provides aggressive warmup and annealing; SWA averages snapshots for better generalization. Implementation: use torch.optim.lr_scheduler.OneCycleLR with optimizer param groups and torch.optim.swa_utils.AveragedModel."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show high validation log-loss (0.474) and training stagnation due to frozen backbone and static learning rate. The initial plan proposes unfreezing with discriminative learning rates and SWA/cyclic LR to improve convergence. Historical successful experiment 1 used a hybrid model with feature engineering but suffered from wasted DeBERTa potential; experiment 2 fine-tuned DeBERTa end-to-end and achieved better results, validating the approach. Thus, the most promising modifications are in 'model_design' (unfreeze backbone with discriminative LR) and 'training_evaluation' (replace scheduler with OneCycleLR+SWA). The data processing is already correct and unchanged.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify SpookyAuthorClassifier to unfreeze DeBERTa backbone layers with discriminative learning rates: unfreeze last 6 layers (lr=1e-5), middle 12 layers (lr=5e-6), bottom 6 layers (lr=1e-6), keep custom head at 5e-5. This allows the pretrained model to adapt to author-specific stylistic patterns (e.g., Poe's Gothic vocabulary, Lovecraft's cosmic horror), as seen in historical experiment 2 where full fine-tuning improved performance. Implementation: set requires_grad selectively by layer index using backbone's layer list.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace cosine scheduler with OneCycleLR (max_lr=2e-5 for backbone, pct_start=0.3, final_div_factor=1e3) and add Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) starting at epoch 15 with swa_lr=5e-6, snapshot every 5 epochs. This addresses training stagnation (LR constant at 2e-5) and validation oscillation, as historical experiment 2 used a warmup scheduler successfully. OneCycleLR provides aggressive warmup and annealing; SWA averages snapshots for better generalization. Implementation: use torch.optim.lr_scheduler.OneCycleLR with optimizer param groups and torch.optim.swa_utils.AveragedModel."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan identifies severe overfitting from epoch 3 onward (training loss near zero, validation loss increasing). Similar historical experiments show that a cohesive fine-tuning pipeline with DeBERTa-v3-large, careful regularization, and additional features works best. The three proposed changes (Multi-Sample Dropout, statistical features, cosine annealing with warmup) directly address overfitting from complementary angles. They should be applied in the training_evaluation and model_design modules; data_processing_and_feature_engineering is also needed to extract the new features. This triad leverages historical success of combining transformer with engineered features and robust training schedules.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Extract statistical features as per initial plan: text length, word count, punctuation density, capitalization ratio, and stopword ratio from each text. Compute these during dataset creation and store them in a separate tensor (or as additional features in the dataset). Preserve existing text and label processing; the new features will be concatenated with the pooled output in the model design module.", "model_design": "Modify the model architecture to incorporate statistical features. After obtaining the hidden states from DeBERTa, take the [CLS] token embedding (or pooled output). Compute statistical features from the input (e.g., using a separate feature extraction call) and pass through a small MLP (e.g., linear layer non-linearity linear) to get 64-dim representation. Concatenate this with the pooled output and feed into the classifier head. Implement Multi-Sample Dropout: during forward pass for training, run the classifier head 4 times with different dropout masks, average the logits before computing loss. Use existing dropout probabilities (0.1). Keep label smoothing and loss function as before but compute loss on averaged logits.", "training_evaluation": "Replace linear scheduler with cosine annealing scheduler with linear warmup for 10% of steps, decay to min_lr=1e-6. Train using Multi-Sample Dropout (forward pass multiple times per batch to compute loss). Monitor validation loss with clipping as before. Save best model based on validation log loss. All other training parameters (epochs, batch size, mixed precision) remain unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan identifies severe overfitting from epoch 3 onward (training loss near zero, validation loss increasing). Similar historical experiments show that a cohesive fine-tuning pipeline with DeBERTa-v3-large, careful regularization, and additional features works best. The three proposed changes (Multi-Sample Dropout, statistical features, cosine annealing with warmup) directly address overfitting from complementary angles. They should be applied in the training_evaluation and model_design modules; data_processing_and_feature_engineering is also needed to extract the new features. This triad leverages historical success of combining transformer with engineered features and robust training schedules.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Extract statistical features as per initial plan: text length, word count, punctuation density, capitalization ratio, and stopword ratio from each text. Compute these during dataset creation and store them in a separate tensor (or as additional features in the dataset). Preserve existing text and label processing; the new features will be concatenated with the pooled output in the model design module.",
    "model_design": "Modify the model architecture to incorporate statistical features. After obtaining the hidden states from DeBERTa, take the [CLS] token embedding (or pooled output). Compute statistical features from the input (e.g., using a separate feature extraction call) and pass through a small MLP (e.g., linear layer non-linearity linear) to get 64-dim representation. Concatenate this with the pooled output and feed into the classifier head. Implement Multi-Sample Dropout: during forward pass for training, run the classifier head 4 times with different dropout masks, average the logits before computing loss. Use existing dropout probabilities (0.1). Keep label smoothing and loss function as before but compute loss on averaged logits.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace linear scheduler with cosine annealing scheduler with linear warmup for 10% of steps, decay to min_lr=1e-6. Train using Multi-Sample Dropout (forward pass multiple times per batch to compute loss). Monitor validation loss with clipping as before. Save best model based on validation log loss. All other training parameters (epochs, batch size, mixed precision) remain unchanged."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The validation logloss shows oscillations (0.349→0.306→0.341) after epoch 4, indicating noisy gradients and overfitting. The initial plan proposes gradient accumulation, multi-scale window attention with BiLSTM, progressive freezing, and synonym augmentation. Similar historical experiments show that a hybrid model combining DeBERTa with engineered features and multi-sample dropout succeeded, while plain LightGBM on sparse features failed due to poor signal-to-noise ratio. Applying changes to model_design and training_evaluation addresses the core issues: model_design adds BiLSTM+attentive pooling to capture sequential stylistic patterns that CLS pooling misses, and training_evaluation implements gradient accumulation and progressive freezing to stabilize training and regularize against overfitting. Data augmentation is not prioritized due to historical mixed results and potential noise.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current [CLS] token pooling with a learnable multi-head attentive pooling over the entire last hidden state sequence. Append a 2-layer BiLSTM (hidden size 256) on top of DeBERTa embeddings before pooling to capture sequential syntactic patterns. The BiLSTM output (concatenated forward+backward) is pooled via multi-head attention (4 heads, 256-dim). Concatenate this pooled representation with the stylometric feature projection (keep existing stylo_proj MLP). Update classifier input dimension accordingly. This enhances induction for authorship attribution by emphasizing clause-level structures common in horror fiction. Keep existing stylometric feature extraction unchanged.", "training_evaluation": "Implement gradient accumulation with 2 micro-batches to simulate an effective batch size of 16, using GradScaler for dynamic mixed precision. Adopt progressive freezing: freeze embeddings and first 8 transformer layers for epochs 1-2, then unfreeze 4 layers every subsequent epoch. Set DeBERTa backbone learning rate to 1e-5 (from 1.5e-5) and head/mlp learning rate to 5e-4. Apply synonym replacement (30% probability per word) and random word dropout (10%) on training data only. These changes stabilize training dynamics, reduce overfitting, and improve generalization capacity."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The validation logloss shows oscillations (0.349→0.306→0.341) after epoch 4, indicating noisy gradients and overfitting. The initial plan proposes gradient accumulation, multi-scale window attention with BiLSTM, progressive freezing, and synonym augmentation. Similar historical experiments show that a hybrid model combining DeBERTa with engineered features and multi-sample dropout succeeded, while plain LightGBM on sparse features failed due to poor signal-to-noise ratio. Applying changes to model_design and training_evaluation addresses the core issues: model_design adds BiLSTM+attentive pooling to capture sequential stylistic patterns that CLS pooling misses, and training_evaluation implements gradient accumulation and progressive freezing to stabilize training and regularize against overfitting. Data augmentation is not prioritized due to historical mixed results and potential noise.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current [CLS] token pooling with a learnable multi-head attentive pooling over the entire last hidden state sequence. Append a 2-layer BiLSTM (hidden size 256) on top of DeBERTa embeddings before pooling to capture sequential syntactic patterns. The BiLSTM output (concatenated forward+backward) is pooled via multi-head attention (4 heads, 256-dim). Concatenate this pooled representation with the stylometric feature projection (keep existing stylo_proj MLP). Update classifier input dimension accordingly. This enhances induction for authorship attribution by emphasizing clause-level structures common in horror fiction. Keep existing stylometric feature extraction unchanged.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement gradient accumulation with 2 micro-batches to simulate an effective batch size of 16, using GradScaler for dynamic mixed precision. Adopt progressive freezing: freeze embeddings and first 8 transformer layers for epochs 1-2, then unfreeze 4 layers every subsequent epoch. Set DeBERTa backbone learning rate to 1e-5 (from 1.5e-5) and head/mlp learning rate to 5e-4. Apply synonym replacement (30% probability per word) and random word dropout (10%) on training data only. These changes stabilize training dynamics, reduce overfitting, and improve generalization capacity."
  }
}"}

