{"reason": "The current code uses a large DeBERTa-v3-large model (304M params) with engineered stylometric and n-gram features, but it overfits severely (train loss 0.31 vs val loss 0.59 by epoch 6) and achieves only 0.3132 log loss. The initial plan proposes replacing the neural model with a gradient boosting ensemble (LightGBM) on the rich feature set to better match the small dataset size. Historical experiments show that pure LightGBM on many features works well when the dataset is small; also, early stopping and ensembling prevent overfitting. The current code already performs extensive feature engineering, so the most promising changes are in model_design (replace neural with boosting ensemble) and training_evaluation (implement cross-validation and ensemble inference). Data processing is already complete and does not need modification.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the HybridAuthorshipModel (DeBERTa + MLP) with a LightGBM ensemble. Specifically, train 4 LightGBM classifiers with different feature subsets: Model1 on all 3579 features; Model2 on top 100 features by mutual information; Model3 on stylometric + char n-grams (579 features); Model4 on word n-grams + TF-IDF (3000 features). Use parameters: learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, num_leaves=64, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.6, min_child_samples=5, 2000 iterations with early stopping (100 rounds). Ensemble predictions by averaging probabilities and applying softmax scaling. This directly addresses the overfitting issue by using smaller, regularized models that suit the 14K training samples, as confirmed by historical successes with boosting on this dataset.", "training_evaluation": "Implement stratified 5-fold cross-validation for LightGBM training, using the same folds as in the current code. For each fold, train the 4 model variants on the training portion, evaluate log loss on the validation portion, and store fold-level predictions. After all folds, compute out-of-fold validation log loss. For the test set, average predictions across all folds and models. Use early stopping on validation log loss within each model to prevent overfitting. Replace the neural training loop entirely: no GPU needed, all CPU-based. Save submission as before. This change aligns with historical experiments where LightGBM with cross-validation outperformed deep learning on this dataset."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current code uses a large DeBERTa-v3-large model (304M params) with engineered stylometric and n-gram features, but it overfits severely (train loss 0.31 vs val loss 0.59 by epoch 6) and achieves only 0.3132 log loss. The initial plan proposes replacing the neural model with a gradient boosting ensemble (LightGBM) on the rich feature set to better match the small dataset size. Historical experiments show that pure LightGBM on many features works well when the dataset is small; also, early stopping and ensembling prevent overfitting. The current code already performs extensive feature engineering, so the most promising changes are in model_design (replace neural with boosting ensemble) and training_evaluation (implement cross-validation and ensemble inference). Data processing is already complete and does not need modification.",
  "module": [
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
  ],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the HybridAuthorshipModel (DeBERTa + MLP) with a LightGBM ensemble. Specifically, train 4 LightGBM classifiers with different feature subsets: Model1 on all 3579 features; Model2 on top 100 features by mutual information; Model3 on stylometric + char n-grams (579 features); Model4 on word n-grams + TF-IDF (3000 features). Use parameters: learning_rate=0.03, max_depth=6, num_leaves=64, subsample=0.8, colsample_bytree=0.6, min_child_samples=5, 2000 iterations with early stopping (100 rounds). Ensemble predictions by averaging probabilities and applying softmax scaling. This directly addresses the overfitting issue by using smaller, regularized models that suit the 14K training samples, as confirmed by historical successes with boosting on this dataset.",
    "training_evaluation": "Implement stratified 5-fold cross-validation for LightGBM training, using the same folds as in the current code. For each fold, train the 4 model variants on the training portion, evaluate log loss on the validation portion, and store fold-level predictions. After all folds, compute out-of-fold validation log loss. For the test set, average predictions across all folds and models. Use early stopping on validation log loss within each model to prevent overfitting. Replace the neural training loop entirely: no GPU needed, all CPU-based. Save submission as before. This change aligns with historical experiments where LightGBM with cross-validation outperformed deep learning on this dataset."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current LightGBM ensemble achieves ~0.59 log loss, plateauing as the initial plan described. The initial plan proposes replacing LightGBM with a pretrained transformer (DistilRoBERTa) to capture syntactic structure and sequential dependencies, which is supported by successful historical experiments showing that a cohesive DeBERTa-v3-large fine-tuning pipeline (with proper regularization and engineered features) can reach much lower log loss. The failed experiments confirm that pure feature engineering and LightGBM cannot capture stylistic DNA. Thus, the most promising changes are: (1) model_design: replace the LightGBM ensemble with a DistilRoBERTa-based transformer (as per initial plan), but incorporate successful historical additions like Multi-Sample Dropout and statistical features to prevent overfitting; (2) training_evaluation: implement a single stratified train/validation split with AdamW, linear warmup, early stopping, and cosine annealing schedule to stabilize training; (3) data_processing_and_feature_engineering: add statistical features (text length, word count, punctuation density, etc.) to complement the transformer, as historical successes show combining engineered features with transformer representations yields better results.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add statistical feature extraction during dataset creation: compute text length (char count), word count, punctuation density (ratio of punctuation to total characters), capitalization ratio (proportion of capitalized words), stopword ratio (proportion of common stopwords), and average word length. Store these as an additional tensor (e.g., 'stat_features') in the dataset, preserving existing text cleaning and label encoding. This enriches the transformer representation with stylometric signals that historical experiments successfully combined with pretrained models.", "model_design": "Replace the LightGBM ensemble with a DistilRoBERTa model (distilroberta-base) for its smaller size and speed. Modify the model to accept both tokenized input IDs/attention masks and a separate statistical feature vector. After obtaining the pooled output (CLS token) from DistilRoBERTa, pass the statistical features through a small MLP (linear->ReLU->linear, output 64 dimensions), concatenate with the pooled output (768+64=832), and then feed into the final classifier head (832->256->3 with GELU activation and 0.2 dropout). During training, implement Multi-Sample Dropout (4 forward passes with different dropout masks, average logits before loss computation) to regularize, as successful historical experiments did. Keep cross-entropy loss with label smoothing (0.1).", "training_evaluation": "Replace the 5-fold CV training loop with a single stratified 80/20 split (as per initial plan). Use AdamW optimizer (lr=2e-5), linear warmup over 10% of steps, and cosine annealing schedule decaying to 1e-6 (from successful experiments). Train for 8 epochs with batch size 32, early stopping on validation log loss (patience=3). Use mixed precision (AMP) to speed up. Save the best model checkpoint. For inference, load the best model, run on test set with no gradient computation, apply softmax to logits, and save submission. This addresses overfitting and follows the successful training recipe from historical experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current LightGBM ensemble achieves ~0.59 log loss, plateauing as the initial plan described. The initial plan proposes replacing LightGBM with a pretrained transformer (DistilRoBERTa) to capture syntactic structure and sequential dependencies, which is supported by successful historical experiments showing that a cohesive DeBERTa-v3-large fine-tuning pipeline (with proper regularization and engineered features) can reach much lower log loss. The failed experiments confirm that pure feature engineering and LightGBM cannot capture stylistic DNA. Thus, the most promising changes are: (1) model_design: replace the LightGBM ensemble with a DistilRoBERTa-based transformer (as per initial plan), but incorporate successful historical additions like Multi-Sample Dropout and statistical features to prevent overfitting; (2) training_evaluation: implement a single stratified train/validation split with AdamW, linear warmup, early stopping, and cosine annealing schedule to stabilize training; (3) data_processing_and_feature_engineering: add statistical features (text length, word count, punctuation density, etc.) to complement the transformer, as historical successes show combining engineered features with transformer representations yields better results.",
  "module": [
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering",
    "model_design",
    "training_evaluation"
